//! Pinned harmonypy reference values for Harmony (§7.4, ORG-7.21-4).
//!
//! GENERATED FILE — do not edit by hand. See `# Regenerating` below.
//!
//! # Why an external reference at all
//!
//! `harmony/tests.rs` is entirely self-consistency: soft assignments sum
//! to one, O and E agree, the arrowhead inverse matches an LU of the same
//! matrix, the same seed gives the same bytes. None of it could see that
//! the soft k-means sub-loop had **no M-step** — `y` and `dist_mat` were
//! frozen for the whole loop, so the E-step could only trade `Σ R·dist`
//! against the entropy penalty at fixed centroids. The one test that
//! compared against a third party (`test_harmony_validation.py`) skips
//! whenever its gitignored 50 MB `.npz` is absent, which is always in CI.
//!
//! # Provenance
//!
//! | constant | produced by |
//! |---|---|
//! | `HP_Z`, `HP_LABELS` | this generator's RNG — the fixture INPUT |
//! | `HP_FIX_*` | harmonypy `Harmony.init_cluster` |
//! | `HP_MSTEP_Y`, `HP_MSTEP_DIST` | harmonypy `Harmony.cluster` |
//! | `HP_RIDGE_Z_CORR` | harmonypy `Harmony.moe_correct_ridge` |
//! | `HP_SOFTMAX_R` | harmonypy `Harmony.update_R` at `theta = 0` |
//! | `HP_OBJ_*` | harmonypy `Harmony.compute_objective` |
//!
//! harmonypy 0.2.0
//! torch 2.11.0
//! numpy 2.4.4
//! scikit-learn 1.8.0
//!
//! # Conventions, and how the two layouts line up
//!
//! harmonypy holds `Z` and `Y` as `(d × N)` / `(d × K)` torch tensors and
//! `R` / `dist_mat` as `(K × N)`. SCX holds `z_orig` column-major `d × N`
//! (`z[i*d + t]`), `y` column-major `d × K` (`y[ku*d + t]`), and `r` /
//! `dist_mat` row-major `K × N`. So a harmonypy `(d × ·)` matrix is
//! emitted **transposed** — one source row per column — and a `(K × N)`
//! one is emitted as-is. The tests flatten in that order and nowhere else.
//!
//! # The bars, and why they are not tighter
//!
//! harmonypy 0.2.0 is torch **float32** end to end (`allocate_buffers`),
//! while SCX accumulates in f64 and stores `r` / `dist_mat` as f32. The
//! parity arms are therefore f32-reference comparisons and cannot be
//! pinned at `abs=0`; each `*_ATOL` below carries the value observed when
//! this file was generated, and is that value rounded up one decimal
//! order. Nothing here is calibrated from what SCX returns.
//!
//! # Two divergences are pinned AS divergences
//!
//! SCX's diversity penalty is `((2E+1)/(O+E+1))^θ` where harmonypy 0.2.0
//! uses `(E/(O+E))^θ`, and SCX's objective cross-entropy is
//! `log((O+E+1)/(2E+1))` where harmonypy uses `log((O+E)/E)`. The factor
//! of 2 cancels in `update_R` (it is constant across `k` for a fixed cell,
//! so the per-cell L1 normalization removes it); the `+1` smoothing does
//! not, and in the objective the factor survives as a `−log(2)·Σ σ O θ`
//! term. Both forms are self-consistent and GPU-matched, so they are
//! recorded with their measured magnitude rather than changed.
//!
//! The **penalty** divergence cannot be pinned against harmonypy directly:
//! `update_R` shuffles cells and updates O/E block by block, and the two
//! implementations draw that permutation from different RNGs. Forcing one
//! block removes every cell at once, which sends O and E to zero and drops
//! harmonypy into its `clamp(E/(O+E), min=1e-8)` floor — its R stops
//! summing to one entirely. So [`HP_OBJ_CROSS`] carries the divergence for
//! the whole penalty family (same ratio, inverted, and deterministic), and
//! [`HP_SOFTMAX_R`] pins the half of `update_R` that has no penalty in it.
//!
//! # Regenerating
//!
//! ```text
//! .venv/bin/python benchmarks/scripts/generate_harmony_references.py \
//!     > scx-accel/src/harmony/harmony_reference_values.rs
//! ```

